why 1 0 RANGE The bass starts high, leaping up to D and stepping down D–C♯–B–A: a light opening register before it descends.
why 1 1/8 RANGE Still the high start; the bass is back on A by the next beat.
why 6 0 LEADING_TONE A deceptive move to F♯ minor (vi): the soprano takes A, so the alto's G♯ steps down to F♯ and comes straight back to G♯, as a neighbor.
why 8 0 PROGRESSION The bass walks down G♯–F♯–E–D toward the final cadence; V⁶ and IV⁶ are passing chords on that line.
why 9 0 SIMILAR_MOTION The final cadence: every voice falls into the A chord, the alto's G♯ to E, the seventh D to C♯ and the bass to A.
